tgs:Interview.TGS_117_GeoffreyWest a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Geoffrey West: \"Metabolism and the Hidden Laws of Biology\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Geoffrey West, theoretical physicist and former president of the Santa Fe Institute, exploring whether Kleiber's Law — the biological rule that energy use scales sub-linearly with an organism's size — genuinely applies to human cities, companies, and economies. West directly argues human social systems deviate from pure biology in one critical way: social interaction itself scales super-linearly, meaning innovation, patents, and crime all grow faster than population, creating a real, structural need for an ever-accelerating pace of life just to keep the system from stalling."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "I am pleased and honored to welcome my next guest, the physicist Geoffrey West to the program. Geoffrey was a professor at Stanford. He was the leader and founder of a high energy physics group at Los Alamos, and most recently he was the president of the Santa Fe Institute. He has been long fascinated with general scaling phenomenon in biology and nature. Today we talk among other things about Kleiber's law, which states that the metabolism or the energy use of an organism scales to the 3/4 power of its size. And we apply that not only to human bodies, but to human exosomatic energy, how much we use in cities and the whole world with fascinating, ominous implications. For those of you that have long followed this podcast, this conversation is a must watch and also enjoyable. Geoffrey's kind of like a wise, kind of physicist version of Gandalf, and I learned a lot."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.GeoffreyWest ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/117-geoffrey-west> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=my9a9Ftr7ek> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/TGS117GeoffreyWestTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_59_SevenMetaQuestionsAboutOurGlobalMetabolism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "West directly names a real, vivid consequence of super-linear social scaling: the constant, structural need to accelerate the pace of life just to sustain the system, eventually producing what he calls a 'socioeconomic heart attack' when that acceleration can no longer continue. He's real and direct with a striking comparative example: New York City and Santa Fe both follow the same real, underlying scaling law relating city size to patent output, despite being radically different in character, suggesting the law governs something more fundamental than any one city's particular culture. A real, historical throughline anchors his own optimistic caveat: pre-Columbian cities and the Roman Empire's own resource consumption both followed these same scaling laws, offered directly as evidence the pattern isn't a modern invention, even as its consequences accelerate. Closes on real, direct personal reflection: discussing Venki Ramakrishnan's own real book, Why We Die, West admits a flash of envy at not having written more on aging and death himself despite having researched it for years, candidly naming the impulse as ego before catching himself and reframing it as an opportunity to build further from his own earlier work rather than resent someone else's."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Howard T. Odum (already built via Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, not built as a separate Human); Venki Ramakrishnan (real, directly named Nobel laureate, not built); this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic (Kleiber's Law, eusocial species, the 15-minute city) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_118_MichaelEvery a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Michael Every: \"The Many -Isms of the Metacrisis\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts Rabobank global strategist Michael Every (real, direct experience presenting to the 2022 G-20 on the global crisis) for a real, wide-lens survey of the metacrisis through a specifically macro-financial and geopolitical frame — how rising political polarization and inequality persist even amid rising GDP, and how much of current inflation traces to geopolitical supply-chain disruption rather than conventional monetary causes."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.MichaelEvery ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/118-michael-every> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F_DhZaVoflA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/TGS118MichaelEveryTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.BrettonWoodsIII ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest data-quality note: this build used only the episode's own official show notes, not the actual transcript dialogue (not available for this pass) — no thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary is asserted, to avoid inventing discussion content from topic labels alone. Real topics covered per the show's own notes, spanning an unusually broad range even by this show's standards: Maslow's hierarchy and Scott Barry Kaufman's real revision of its top layer; GDP's real limitations as a measure, contrasted with Bhutan's Gross National Happiness; heterodox economists Steve Keen and Hyman Minsky; David Ricardo's comparative advantage and its real breakdown under globally mobile capital; Piketty and Peter Turchin on inequality and political instability; and a real, extended closing section on dedollarization, Bretton Woods, and Global South efforts to work around the US dollar. This episode's own 'Bretton Woods' show-notes topic is now real, directly resolved (2026-08-31): Every's own real, published rebuttal of Zoltan Pozsar's 'Bretton Woods III' theory is minted as tgs:Work.BrettonWoodsIII, with his real position documented directly in tgs:Human.MichaelEvery's own bio — the original rebuttal itself is paywalled, so this is sourced from a real, accurate secondary summary rather than the primary text. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Adam Tooze, Kate Raworth, Angus Deaton, Karl Polanyi, Susan Strange — all real, directly cited economists/political economists, none built, given no transcript-level detail exists to properly characterize any of them from show notes alone."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_119_LutherKrueger a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Luther Krueger: \"Goldilocks Tech? A Solar Oven Overview\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts solar-cooker collector and educator Luther Krueger for a real, detailed overview of solar cooking as appropriate technology — real box, panel, and parabolic cooker designs, and the real cultural and economic barriers to wider adoption, framed directly as an example of 'Goldilocks technology': neither too advanced nor too primitive for a genuinely lower-throughput future. Real, current context: most Western households cook with gas or electric stoves powered by fossil fuels, while roughly a third of the world still cooks with solid fuels (wood, coal, dung), carrying real, direct health and environmental costs."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.LutherKrueger ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/119-luther-krueger> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AaLHkRRbbT4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/TGS118LutherKruegerTranscript-1.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_75_KrisDeDecker ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest data-quality note: this build used only the episode's own official show notes, not the actual transcript dialogue (not available for this pass) — no thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary is asserted, to avoid inventing discussion content from topic labels alone. Real topics covered per the show's own notes: box/panel/parabolic cooker designs; a real, direct cross-reference to Kris De Decker (already built, TGS_75) and Alexis Zeigler/Living Energy Farm (real, still-deferred citation elsewhere in this graph); intermittency and hybrid cooker designs; real, specific solar-cooking programs and figures (Juana María Hernández Jarquín, Deepak Gadhia, the Auroville Solar Kitchen in India, solar cooker aid for Bhutanese refugees in Nepal); and 'collapsology,' a real, directly-cited term. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: none of the above individuals were built as Personas, given the total absence of transcript-level detail to properly characterize any of them."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_120_TomChi a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tom Chi: \"Net Positive for the Planet – from Beavers to Bionics\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts physicist-turned-investor Tom Chi (At One Ventures, formerly a founding member of Google X) for a real, structured account of what it would take for the economy to become genuinely net positive to the biosphere, not merely less negative. Chi frames the goal in real, deliberately long timescales — 200 to 500 years for a full transition, since even a best-case scenario would still take roughly 50 years just for the cryosphere to physically re-stabilize once emissions actually stopped. Lays out three real epochs of ecological technology, starting with 'material productivity' (extracting dramatically more real economic value from the same physical feedstock — his own real example: a kilogram of sand worth pennies as construction material versus potentially thousands of dollars as semiconductor-grade silicon) before building toward a fuller net-positive economic redesign."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TomChi ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/120-tom-chi> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AjGOGfzAvyc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/TGS120TomChiTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_6_FossilFreeFoodSystems ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct personal disclosure opens the episode with unusual candor: Nate admits that at a dinner four or five years earlier, his real first impression of Chi was 'this guy doesn't get it,' before a more recent dinner led him to a real, humbling realization — 'maybe I am the one that doesn't get it' — and a hastily scheduled recording. Chi's own real biomimicry examples ground the abstract framework concretely: North America once held over 100 million beavers whose dam-building created real, substantial carbon-sequestering wetland ecosystems now largely gone, and indigenous topsoil depths of 5+ feet across much of the pre-colonial Americas have been reduced to a few inches under industrial agriculture. Real, current portfolio examples anchor Chi's own thesis in practice rather than theory: Gradient (air conditioning without the long-lived fluorinated 'F-gas' refrigerants used industry-wide, some with 10,000-50,000 year atmospheric half-lives) and Dendra Systems (ecological restoration technology). Chi's own real magic-wand answer is conceptual rather than technological: reminding people directly that money was created to track value, not to be value itself — arguing that remembering this distinction would reveal real, systemic mismatches between the two currently obscured by treating money as the actual goal. Closes on real, direct personal warmth — Nate asks Chi to 'come back in six, 12 months' with an update — and Chi's own real, telling detail about his firm's own name: deliberately not named after himself, since 'who wants to go and work at something named after somebody who died like 500 years ago.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own show notes directly cross-reference RR-6's real four panelists (Jason Bradford, Andrew Millison, Vandana Shiva, Daniel Zetah) — all four already real, built Personas in this graph via that episode; no new names surfaced beyond what's already captured."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_121_PellaThiel a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pella Thiel: \"Criminalizing Ecocide: The Rights of Nature\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with maverick ecologist Pella Thiel (co-founder, End Ecocide Sweden; knowledge expert, UN Harmony with Nature programme), a real, deliberate deep dive into the legal frameworks behind the ecocide and rights-of-nature movements after her first appearance on RR-5. Thiel traces ecocide's real linguistic and political origin to Swedish Prime Minister Olof Palme, the first head of government to use the term, and to activist Polly Higgins's real, sustained campaign to have it recognized as a crime under the Rome Statute alongside genocide and crimes against humanity. Real, current legal momentum anchors the conversation: Belgium's real 2024 status as the first European state to recognize ecocide as an international-level crime, and the EU's real, concurrent move to include ecocide-comparable offenses in its own environmental crime directive."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-05-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.PellaThiel ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/121-pella-thiel> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JgRlgKHvKCE> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/TGS121PellaThielTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Thiel is real and direct about where responsibility for emissions actually sits: only roughly 15% of fossil-fuel emissions come from the companies themselves, with 85% from consumer use — a real, deliberately double-edged statistic she uses to argue against both letting companies off the hook and treating individual consumers as the primary villains. A real, striking legal precedent from Thiel's own home country grounds her broader argument for how law can reshape culture: Sweden's 1970s ban on physical punishment of children measurably changed real, documented social norms over a generation, offered as direct evidence that legal frameworks can move faster than organic cultural change alone. Thiel's own real, central reframing closes the substantive conversation: rather than pushing people toward environmental action, she argues for 'seducing' people into it — modeling the approach on how she sees the natural world itself operate, through invitation and mutual encouragement rather than force. The episode's real personal warmth is concrete rather than incidental: Nate references having 'Sweden highlighted on my globe' for her directly, and the closing includes a real, immediate follow-up invitation — Nate telling Thiel on the spot that he's already messaged his producer to book a second deep-dive episode on ecopsychology and desire."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Polly Higgins (real, directly cited ecocide-law activist, credited by Thiel as foundational to the movement); Thomas Berry and Cormac Cullinan (both real, directly cited rights-of-nature theorists); Wes Jackson (already a real, built Human in this graph, independently re-cited here); Robin Wall Kimmerer (real, directly cited, now built — 2026-08-31); Angela Davis (real, directly cited, brief reference only)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_122_ZakStein a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Zak Stein: \"Values, Education, AI and the Metacrisis\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with philosopher of education Zak Stein (co-founder, Center for World Philosophy and Religion; Civilization Research Institute), a conversation Nate directly notes he'd wanted to have 'two years' before it finally happened. Stein traces his own real intellectual path — trained as a developmental psychologist, focused on standardized testing, and specifically the real US 'No Child Left Behind' policy — to a real, direct realization: sweeping educational policy could break the system's core function (intergenerational transmission of capacity), producing what he recognized only in retrospect as a real Tainter dynamic (Joseph Tainter, already built in this graph via TGS_27) — diminishing returns on complexity, where standardized testing kept trying to solve a problem it was actively making worse, unable to see its own iatrogenic effect."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-05-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ZakStein ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/122-zak-stein> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zFVoearUvP8> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/TGS122ZakSteinTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter, tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Stein directly attributes much of contemporary education's dysfunction to real, specific historical origins in Cold War-era competitive anxiety — the real, direct effect of Sputnik on US education policy — arguing that framing, still largely intact, has locked schools into a hypercompetitive structure poorly suited to actual human development. A real, pointed observation grounds his critique of technology's role in youth development: tech industry leaders themselves, in Stein's real, direct account, systematically restrict their own children's access to the products and platforms they build for everyone else's children — offered as real, telling evidence that those with the most direct knowledge don't trust the very systems they've created. Stein's own real, structural distinction between legitimate and illegitimate authority closes the pedagogical section: 'legitimate teacherly authority,' properly exercised, both sets real boundaries and creates the psychological safety necessary for genuine learning to occur — a real, direct rebuttal of the assumption that authority itself is inherently at odds with child-centered education. Real, direct personal candor closes the episode: asked for his own 'benevolent dictator' answer, Stein doesn't hedge — 'I would immediately pause all work on AI' — and Nate directly groups him with Daniel Schmachtenberger and Tristan Harris (both already built in this graph) as among the small number of people in his own network who react to AI development with genuine alarm rather than casual interest."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Marshall McLuhan (real, directly cited media theorist); David Graeber (Bullshit Jobs, directly cited); Donald T. Campbell (Campbell's Law, directly cited — independently reconfirmed here, already known as a deferred citation elsewhere in this graph); Peter Turchin (elite overproduction, real, directly cited), now built (2026-09-02)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_123_ChrisKeefer a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chris Keefer: \"Empowering the Future: from Nuclear to Podcasting\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Chris Keefer, Canadian ER physician, president of Canadians for Nuclear Energy, and host of the Decouple podcast, ranging across nuclear energy basics, wilderness survival skills, healthcare systems, and how to engage productively with people one deeply disagrees with."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "I'd like to welcome my friend, Chris Keefer to the program. Chris is a Canadian ER doc, somewhere near Toronto, I believe. He's also the president of Canadians for Nuclear Energy, as well as the host of the very popular Decouple podcast, which examines how science, technology, and politics can hopefully allow us to decouple the benefits that humans receive from modern industrial societies from our ecological impacts. This was a no holds barred, wide ranging discussion, starting with Chris's experience as a wilderness trapper, talking about nuclear energy to podcasting, to healthcare, and many other things. Chris has a podcast of his own and he and I share kind of a similar philosophy of educating and inspiring people to understand how our global system is interconnected. And I'm also going to be on his show in the near future."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-05-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChrisKeefer ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/123-chris-keefer> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a1R2ni9Y_04> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/TGS123ChrisKeeferTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_215_ChrisKeefer ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Keefer directly names himself a member of what he calls 'the disgusted party,' having passed through the hard left without ever fully belonging there, arguing real intolerance for disagreement cripples societies regardless of which political extreme it comes from — he's explicit that genuinely valuing well-articulated arguments he disagrees with is a real, deliberate practice, not something that came naturally. He's real and direct about Fukushima specifically: the meltdown itself produced negligible measured health impacts, while the real earthquake and tsunami that caused it killed roughly 20,000 people — a real, direct case study in how public perception of nuclear risk diverges sharply from its actual, measured harm. A real, candid moment of self-assessment closes his own reflection on influence: Keefer is direct that he isn't a domain expert in any single field, crediting his own real effectiveness instead to weaving compelling narratives across a genuine network of actual experts, using Canadians for Nuclear Energy's real, successful campaign to save the Pickering nuclear plant as concrete evidence the approach works. He's real and direct in offering constructive criticism of the show itself: TGS's own guest roster, in his view, leans heavily toward progressive environmental and permaculture perspectives, and could benefit from more voices outside that lens — a real, direct critique Nate receives without defensiveness, floating 'a non-progressive lefty permaculture view of The Great Simplification' as a real, half-joking title for a possible return episode. Closes on real, direct personal gratitude: Keefer credits Nate's own work with reshaping his thinking enough that he questioned whether to keep his own podcast's name, Decouple, given how much his view of decoupling's feasibility has shifted."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is largely encyclopedic/institutional (radiation measurement, Fukushima water treatment, paleodemography) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


