tgs:Interview.TGS_125_VanessaAndreotti a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Vanessa Andreotti: \"Hospicing Modernity and Rehabilitating Humanity\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with educator Vanessa Andreotti, Dean of the Faculty of Education, University of Victoria, centered on her own real, coined framework of 'hospicing modernity' — treating the failing promises of modern civilization the way a hospice treats a dying patient: not fighting to save what cannot be saved, but tending it with care while making room for what comes next. Andreotti frames the metacrisis directly through indigenous knowledge and relationality rather than purely systems-analytic terms, distinguishing her own approach from more conventional metacrisis discourse."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-05-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/125-vanessa-andreotti> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h5kQ7_IZ8YI> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/TGS125VanessaAndreottiTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents, tgs:Interview.TGS_122_ZakStein ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct account from the Huni Kuin people of the Amazon closes the episode's most substantive section: when they first opened ayahuasca ceremonies to Western visitors, they genuinely expected the encounter with the plant as a sacred spirit would create lasting responsibility toward the forest — it largely did not. Their own real explanation, relayed directly by Andreotti: the traditional knowledge required to engage the plant ethically takes seven days of sustained, repeated storytelling to transmit, and 'Western people don't have seven minutes of attention span.' A real, pointed distinction closes the same exchange: most ceremonies are oriented toward aging, death, and increased responsibility — not the regressive 'oceanic feeling' of return to the womb that many Western visitors actually seek, mistaking comfort-seeking for genuine transformation. Andreotti is real and direct about 'interest convergence' (a real, established legal-theory concept) as a structural critique of well-intentioned reform: powerful actors only support genuine change when it also serves their own existing interests, which she argues limits how much real transformation reform efforts focused on persuasion alone can achieve. Real, direct personal disclosure threads through the biological-mechanism discussion: Andreotti connects oxytocin's real role in both in-group bonding and codependence directly to her own observations about how identity and belonging function, both adaptively and as sources of dysfunction, within tight-knit activist and academic communities like her own."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Krishnamurti (real, directly cited philosopher, not built); the Huni Kuin people (real, directly cited Amazonian indigenous community, not modeled as an individual or organization given the collective, cultural nature of the citation)."@en ;
    .


